> Climate change has very wide-ranging economic impacts. Comprehensive
> economic assessments are therefore needed, yet existing estimates
> remain fragmented, incomplete and deeply uncertain. Here we develop
> an emulation framework for a much more complete economic valuation
> of climate risks. We apply it to the UK, which provides a test case because
> of the availability of many estimates from models and expert elicitations.
> The approach provides channel-specific estimates, quantifies climatic
> and socioeconomic uncertainties, disaggregates to 406 local areas and
> crucially includes understudied impacts such as spillovers from other
> regions and missing risks. Although current warming produces welfare
> losses equivalent to 2% of gross domestic product (95% confidence interval
> of 1–4%), damages grow rapidly under the baseline scenario and reach 10% of
> gross domestic product (2–20%) by the end of the century. We demonstrate
> half the expected total damages and the greatest uncertainties come from
> catastrophic and missing risks.
> Climate change has a very wide range of social and economic impacts1,2
> .
> Comprehensive assessments of these impacts are therefore crucial for
> identifying vulnerable groups, prioritizing risk management and adaptation and informing mitigation action3
> . However, existing bottom-up,
> risk-by-risk assessments are fragmented and incomplete. Furthermore, although several attempts have been made to estimate economic
> impacts from several channels and combine them either regionally4,5
> or globally6,7
> , the limited scope of and lack of consistency in sectoral
> coverage (Supplementary Table 3) remain pervasive challenges. This
> contributes to large uncertainty about overall climate damages8,9
> .
> In response to these gaps in the literature, we develop an emulationbased approach to construct a much more comprehensive estimate of
> national climate damages, using sector-specific impact functions and
> covering not only modelled but also missing risks (Fig. 1). Our integration architecture is based on estimating simplified statistical models
> that replicate the outputs of more complex models, allowing these
> to be projected with spatial disaggregation and uncertainty under
> consistent scenarios. In addition, we use the expert elicited indicative
> ranges of damages to calibrate an aggregate ‘missing risks’ estimate,
> using the method described in ref. 10. Missing risks here refers to
> the social, natural, business and infrastructure impacts that are not
> captured by the models included elsewhere in the analysis. Finally, we
> also develop a new estimate of trade spillover risks, representing how
> disruption in the trading partners of a country can propagate through
> trade linkages. We use econometric estimates of gross domestic product (GDP) loss by country and translate them into supply-chain losses
> using computable general equilibrium (CGE) estimates of GDP losses
> from trade barriers.
> The UK serves as a test case for this methodology. The UK is vulnerable to a wide range of climate impacts, including heat stress, coastal,
> fluvial and pluvial flooding, declining crop and fishery yields and biodiversity losses11–13. Moreover, the UK provides one of the greatest
> bodies of knowledge for assessing climate risks, including multimodel
> studies and expert elicitations at both the UK14 and European Union
