> By bringing together detailed model results with these expert assessments, our approach enables comprehensive estimates of national
> climate damages.
> Channel-specific damages
> Damages are reported as the percentage difference in GDP relative
> to a counterfactual scenario with the same socioeconomic evolution
> (using shared socioeconomic pathways, SSPs) but without climate
> impacts (Fig. 2). The estimates comprise both market and non-market
> impacts, the latter of which directly affect utility but are converted into
> an equivalent loss of GDP for aggregation. Thus, the implicit economic
> model is of exogenous growth, combined with direct climate impacts
> on the flow of utility individuals obtain in each time-period and indirect
> impacts via changes to the level of output/income. If several distinct
> models are available for an impact channel, a meta-analysis is reported
> to characterize both the most likely outcome across models and the
> combined uncertainty.
> We consider three time periods: current (2011–2030), mid-century
> (2041–2060) and end-century (2081–2100). We compare the contrasting scenarios ‘baseline’ (modelled as SSP3-7.0) and ‘high mitigation’
> (SSP1-2.6) to elucidate potential economic outcomes and the possible benefits of aggressive mitigation. As we are interested in the
> (EU)15,16 levels. Overall, we incorporate 27 models and two missing risk
> approaches in this way. This corpus includes innovations in climate science and in the economic valuation of market and non-market damages
> and a diverse range of methodologies. We show how to combine such
> a diverse set of estimates and channels to produce a more complete
> analysis. Our approach can be applied to other regions of the world. In
> particular, the evidence is rich enough for much of the EU and for the
> USA. For less-studied regions, a growing body of literature describes
> impacts across the globe, accounting for local heterogeneity and local
> adaptation and complementing region-specific studies to provide a
> basis for similar analyses17–19.
> Bringing together a much larger range of models offers new opportunities to understand the range of uncertainty across methodologies
> and indicate potential biases. The assessment that is closest to our work
> is that of ref. 5, which focuses on the USA (Supplementary Table 4). As
> in our study, they incorporate evidence from several methodologies
> using emulated relationships to produce standardized projections,
> they account for adaptation where possible and they aim for a comprehensive, bottom-up picture. Our study makes several advances on
> this work: (1) we capture climate, socioeconomic and model-based
> uncertainty; (2) we develop an approach to disaggregate results to
