> by 0.5 pp [−0.8 ± 1.9 pp] with high mitigation. Meanwhile, missing risks
> are estimated at 2% by the end of the century under the baseline, with
> a wide uncertainty range (1–5%). High mitigation could reduce these
> losses by 1 pp [−0.3 ± 2 pp]. Among the remaining channels, labour
> productivity is the largest in terms of magnitude (1.2% [0.9 ± 1.4%] at the
> end of the century under the baseline), whereas for all other channels
> expected damages fall in the range 0–1%. Generally, both the estimated
> magnitude of damages and the uncertainty about damages increase
> over time. The greatest uncertainties also come from trade spillovers,
> catastrophic damages and missing risks. A high-mitigation scenario
> results in end-century impacts that are similar to mid-century impacts
> on the baseline scenario.
> These estimates are based on 13 process-based models, 7 econometric models, 4 meta-analyses (excluding our Bayesian combinations)
