> Our main UK-wide results do not capture the welfare consequences
> of regional heterogeneity, but an inequality-averse national government might assign a lower welfare value to scenarios where climate
> damages increase income inequality across regions. Equity weighting
> adjusts national climate damages to reflect this inequality aversion
> by converting expected regional GDP losses into utility (also using
> an elasticity of marginal utility of 1.35), aggregating across regions to
> account for the greater welfare impact of losses in poorer areas and
> then converting the combined welfare loss back into monetary terms.
> In our results, the equity-weighted welfare loss in the baseline scenario
> is 14.5% of GDP by the end of the century, compared with 11.2% without
> equity weighting and the combined effects of inequality and risk aversion together are equivalent to a 15.9% loss of GDP. This compares to
> 7% of GDP in the high-mitigation scenario (Supplementary Section C.1
> gives details of these welfare calculations).
> Regional variations in total damages are shown at the NUTS1 level
> (highest level in the European nomenclature of territorial units, representing major socioeconomic regions (Fig. 3b). These differences are
> driven by several channels, with energy supply and demand and labour
> productivity varying most prominently (Supplementary Sections B.5
> and B.6). London experiences the third-greatest losses, as a fraction
> of local GDP, out of NUTS1 regions, at about 11% of GDP by the end
> of the century under the baseline. By the end of the century, London
> experiences the greatest energy supply and demand damages at 1.3%,
> while the East Midlands leads in labour productivity (1.7%) and health
> damages (0.5%), East of England in agricultural damages (0.8%), South
> East England in coastal (0.7%) and Northern Ireland in livestock and
> fishery (0.1%) (Supplementary Tables 29–34).
> Across all regions, catastrophic impacts represent the greatest
> risk. These do not vary by region, since they are driven by global temperatures in our modelling approach (we do not have the ability to
> predict differences in exposure and vulnerability). Beyond catastrophic
> impacts, different regions are most exposed to different risks (Fig. 4).
> For many regions, the next greatest risk is missing risks. Amongst the
> identified risks, across most of the UK, labour productivity losses and
> increases in energy demand dominate. However, coastal impacts,
> health, livestock and fisheries and agriculture are the channels with
> the greatest impacts in some regions. Losses to agricultural productivity in the hardest-hit ADM3 regions reach 4% of GDP and losses are up
> to 40% in the most extreme outcomes, due to the potential collapse
> of the Atlantic meridional overturning circulation (Supplementary
> Section B.2).
> This ‘greatest risk’ channel is subject to uncertainty. Many of these
> risks have a chance of being the greatest risk across our measures of
> uncertainty (Fig. 4). This is shown by the call-out pie charts. Each pie
> chart corresponds to an ADM3 region and represents the probability
> estimated across Monte Carlo draws that a given channel is the most
> impactful in that ADM3 region under the end-century climatology. This
> uncertainty reflects statistical uncertainty, not variability. The ADM3
> regions were chosen to highlight different patterns: although impacts
> in all regions could be dominated by missing non-catastrophic risks,
> impacts in some regions are more likely to be dominated by labour productivity losses (for example, Birmingham), energy demand increases
> (for example, Glasgow), livestock and fisheries (for example Banbridge
> in Northern Ireland), coastal impacts (for example, Canterbury in South
> East England) or agriculture (for example, fenland, an area of low-lying
> land in the East of England).
> Discussion
> We find that the risks to the UK economy are considerable, reaching
> 10% of UK GDP by the end of the century under the baseline. Across
> the channels, the largest risk factor is catastrophic risk, reflecting the
> potential for global economic disruption, but spillover risks through
> trade, labour productivity losses and missing risks are also large. Transitioning from a baseline scenario (SSP3-7.0) to a high-mitigation scenario
> (SSP1-2.6) provides benefits of 1.4% of GDP by mid-century and 6.5% of
> GDP by the end of the century.
> The analysis presented here has an unprecedented coverage of
> identified and missing risks. In comparison, the sectors covered by the
> US Environmental Protection Agency in its recently updated social cost
> of carbon calculation (health, energy, labour productivity, agriculture
> and coastal damages) represent 29% of our total damages7
> , which
> underscores the importance of including catastrophic risks, missing
> risks and trade spillovers.
> However, the results presented here are only as accurate as the
> underlying models and expert elicitations on which they depend. Each
> impact channel has limited coverage of the complex risk mechanisms
> involved, resulting in likely underestimation (Supplementary Section
> B gives identified limitations). A wide range of mechanisms, including
> most cross-channel, cross-space and cross-time interactions, are not
> explicitly represented. They may be captured by catastrophic and
> missing risks, but to an unknown degree. Future work could integrate
> these channel-specific relationships into CGEs and other macroeconomic models, which are designed to provide these important interactions. We are also missing many adaptation options, as well as the
> associated adaptation costs and frictions. As a result, it is difficult to
> say whether we are more at risk of underestimating or overestimating
> total damages. More research is needed to close data gaps on both
> channel-specific and system-wide risks.
> Our focus has been on developing a method to estimate the economic impacts of climate change consistently across as many impact
> channels as possible and aggregate them. In doing so, we necessarily
> impose assumptions about the data, including on the probability distributions (Gaussian), the structure of damages (typically quadratic,
> with no persistence of damages into future periods) and the broader
> economy (following exogenous, deterministic growth). We also adhere
