> Methods
> Climate data
> Future global temperatures are simulated using the AR6-calibrated FaIR
> model22. We apply SSP1-2.6 and SSP3-7.0 to provide common scenarios
> across all impact estimates. The FaIR model reports the full distribution
> of future global climate uncertainty, described by a collection of 2,237
> Monte Carlo projections of global surface air temperature, as used in
> AR6 (Supplementary Section A.3). We adjust the raw FaIR temperatures
> for the effects of tipping points, based on ref. 23, as described in Supplementary Section A.4.
> Higher-resolution temperature changes are produced using pattern scaling, using nine general circulation models downscaled to 10′
> resolution by ref. 24. We assign a general circulation model-specific
> pattern to each FaIR Monte Carlo run, based on its warming level. This
> process is described in Supplementary Section A.5, with pattern scaling
> calculations in Supplementary Section A.6.
> Impact models
> This work represents a range of cutting-edge studies on impact estimates, which we translate into a consistent evaluation process. This
> includes models from the EU-funded PESETA and COACCH projects,
> referenced below. The specific models and papers used are as follows:
> (1) Droughts and flooding: Hydrological models paired with
> empirical low-flow losses (ISFLOOD from PESETA IV15 for
> drought) or with estimates of runoff, inundation, productivity and infrastructure loss (GLOFRIS and OSDaMage from
> COACCH25 and Future Flood Explorer26 for flooding)
> (2) Agriculture: Process-based models covering major crops (EPIC
> from COACCH27 and WOFOST from PESETA IV15) and an econometric model of arable land and AMOC28
> (3) Livestock and fisheries: Process-based marine fisheries model
> DBEM from COACCH27 and threshold-based models for milk
> production, lamb production and inland fisheries29
> (4) Ecosystems: Combination of a custom calibration from IPCC
> AR4 WGII30 for biodiversity loss, G4M from COACCH27 for
> forestland amenities and ref. 31 for wood production
> (5) Energy supply and demand: Econometric models from ref. 19
> and COACCH32 for electricity and other energy demand;
> process-based POLES model from PESETA IV15 and an econometric model from COACCH32 for wind and hydropower supply
> (6) Labor productivity: Experiment-based model from PESETA III33
> and an econometric model from COACCH32
> (7) Health: Biophysical model from PESETA IV15 and an econometric
> meta-analysis from ref. 34, both covering heat and cold-related
> mortality
> (8) Coastal impacts: Engineering models of inundation and storm
> surge from CIAM35 and DIVA25
> (9) Catastrophic risk: Catastrophic component of a meta-analysis
> of global damage functions20
> Trade effects and missing risks are treated differently and described
> below.
> A variety of methods are used in the underlying studies to calculate impacts and economic losses, including process-based and
> statistical modelling approaches. To re-project these results under
> our temperature scenarios, we create emulation models capturing
> heterogeneity and uncertainty. Emulators are calibrated to local
> temperatures from the same climate data as the original study, with
> a separate emulator for each region reported in the original study.
> Quadratic functions of local temperature are typically used for the
> emulators. This is described in Supplementary Sections A.7 and A.8.
> We also project econometric models that describe the top-down risk
> of climate to economic growth from refs. 36–38 using national temperatures for the globe. These are used to study the spillover effects
> from damages elsewhere.
> For some channels, several study estimates are included. In
> these cases, we perform a meta-analysis using a hierarchical Bayesian
> model39, aimed at providing an improved best-estimate. This is
> described in Supplementary Section A.9.
> Although the risk evaluation developed here is meant to approximate comprehensiveness, it is far from exhaustive. Within each impact
> channel, there are issues that are not captured. The list of channels is
> summarized in Supplementary Tables 1 and 2. Details on methodology
> and data for each channel are included in Supplementary Section B.
> Projection
> We compare a baseline scenario (SSP3-7.0), which reaches 4 °C above
> the early industrial period by 2100, to a high-mitigation scenario
> (SSP1-2.6), which stabilizes global temperatures under 2 °C. Throughout, we report impacts and damages for three periods: current climate
> damages (2011–2030), mid-century (2041–2060) and end-century
> (2081–2100). The first period captures those impacts that are currently
> being experienced, to the extent that they can be attributed to climate
> change within our emulated impact framework.
> The benefits of mitigation are the difference in impacts between
> SSP3-7.0 and SSP1-2.6.
> Emulation functions vary at the resolution projected in the underlying studies and we apply these functions to weather within ADM3
> regions according to the GADM database v.3.4 (https://gadm.org/
> index.html). These consist of 406 regions, with 326 county districts,
> metropolitan boroughs and unitary authorities in England, 26 districts
> in Northern Ireland, 32 unitary districts in Scotland and 22 unitary
> authorities in Wales. The median ADM3 region is 375 km2
> . This is comparable to the resolution of the downscaled climate data used, which
> is about 200 km2
> .
> To ensure consistency between the regional emulators and the
> ADM3-level results produced by them, we scale the ADM3 results by
> a scaling factor for each region so that the weighted sum of ADM3
> results equals the value produced by the regional emulator applied
> to regionally averaged temperatures. Weights depend on the impact
> being evaluated (for example, area planted or population).
> We follow socioeconomic assumptions of underlying studies: in
> some cases, a static economy is assumed; in other cases, population
> and economic growth are included. To minimize the role of these
> distinct socioeconomic growth assumptions, we report results as a
> percentage of (contemporaneous) GDP. Socioeconomic assumptions
> used in reporting results are described in Supplementary Sections A.10
> and A.11.
> Missing risks
> Missing risks are estimated using two approaches. First, we use the
> relatively exhaustive list of channels in CCRA314, each of which was
> assessed very approximately for future time periods with a range (for
> example, £10 million to £100 million). We also use tables from published analyses included in the CCRA3 to estimate a mean and standard
> deviation, where available. We construct a mixed distribution from the
> range and a normal distribution describing the mean and standard
> deviation, clipped at the limits of the range. Where values are missing, we extrapolate from existing values for the same impact when
> possible and randomly draw from other impacts otherwise. We drop
> channels that are already assessed by other models. We also record
> any double-counting between missing risks, according to overlaps
> described in CCRA3 and the underlying hazard (for example, heat
> waves and storms) for each risk. We then apply the method in ref. 10,
> constructing a correlation matrix based on the shared hazards and
> classes of hazards.
> Second, following ref. 40, we estimate losses from missing risks as
> a fraction (25%) of known risks. Since we have several models for most
> channels, we bootstrap this approach, randomly drawing a model from
> each impact channel, summing them and then calculating the missing 
> Nature Climate Change
> Article https://doi.org/10.1038/s41558-026-02665-2
> risks. Our final estimate of missing risks draws randomly from these
> two methods for each climate Monte Carlo run.
